maria: Heads-up — onboarding sample-app is causing a bursty spike in requests when users hit the paginated /embed endpoint. Repro + logs below, might be a client-side handshake/windowing bug.

aj: Can you paste the minimal repro and the exact symptoms? Is this during initial warmup or after token refresh? :thinking:

maria: Repro is simple: run sample-app with default SDK settings (concurrency 8, window 16), walk through onboarding flow that pages embeddings. After ~30s the app ramps to >1k rps for a few seconds, then API returns 429s and the client retries immediately. Logs:```
[2026-03-22T16:02:10Z] client: page=1 start
[2026-03-22T16:02:11Z] client: page=2 start
[2026-03-22T16:02:11Z] http 429 Retry-After: 0
[2026-03-22T16:02:11Z] client: retry immediate page=2
[2026-03-22T16:02:11Z] http 429 Retry-After: 0
[2026-03-22T16:02:11Z] client: retry immediate page=2
...
```
Note the gateway returns Retry-After: 0 (because quota check is async) and our JS SDK treats that as immediate retry, which triggers a tight retry loop and then the prefix cache warmup causes bursty behavior.

sam_g: Ah — we've seen something like this: if Retry-After is 0 we currently use immediate retry with exponential backoff base=0 => effectively no delay. Should treat 0 as a sentinel for 'short default' and add jitter. I think the root is the handshake that supplies window permits. When a page fails we immediately release permits back, then other paginated cursors grab them and hammer again.

leo: Proposed short-term mitigations: 1) SDK: clamp Retry-After minimum to 500ms and apply randomized jitter (±200ms) 2) Change permit return semantics: mark returned permits as "cooldown" for 1s instead of immediately reusable 3) Sample-app: reduce default window to 4 for onboarding flows.

aj: Can you sketch the JS change so I can review?

maria: quick patch I tested locally — reduced bursts in my run from ~1.2k rps peak to ~180 rps and eliminated the tight retry loop. Patch (pseudo):
```js
// client/backoff.js
function parseRetryAfter(header) {
  const v = Number(header || 0);
  if (!Number.isFinite(v) || v <= 0) return 500 + Math.floor(Math.random()*400); // min 500ms + jitter
  return Math.max(500, v*1000) + Math.floor(Math.random()*200) // server secs -> ms
}

// token window: when returning permits, mark them with cooldown
function returnPermits(permits) {
  permits.forEach(p => {
    p.cooldownUntil = Date.now() + 1000; // 1s cooldown
    cooldownQueue.push(p);
  });
}

// re-inject from cooldownQueue periodically
setInterval(() => {
  while (cooldownQueue.length && cooldownQueue[0].cooldownUntil <= Date.now()) {
    availablePool.push(cooldownQueue.shift())
  }
}, 100)
```

sam_g: Nice — two notes: (a) use server-suggested Retry-After if >=1s (don't override) (b) add a max backoff cap (30s) in case of sustained quota.

leo: For the sample app defaults, I'll open a tiny PR to set window to 4 and add a comment explaining why for onboarding init flows. Also add a small integration that verifies we don't see >3x baseline during synthetic paging.

maria: I'll open the SDK PR with unit tests for parseRetryAfter and an e2e that reproduces the loop. Assigning: @aj please review the token-return semantics, @sam_g please review backoff bounds. I'll also add a staging job that runs the sample-app against the hosted catalog to catch this in CI.

